@meta
id: 11-02
title: What counts as a change
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat
par: 19
minutes: 4
vscode: four separate double-click-and-delete trips
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
Four entries in this changelog still carry the `WIP ` prefix they were
drafted with. The release is cut, so the prefixes go. The other lines are
finished entries and must not move.

The four are not next to each other, so between one deletion and the next
you have to travel. The question this lesson answers is whether travelling
costs you the repeat.

It does not. `.` remembers the last *change* — the last thing that
altered the text. Cursor movement is not a change. Neither is writing the
file. You can wander as far as you like and `.` is still loaded with the
same edit.

@keys
| `dw` | delete a word, including the space after it |
| `.` | repeat the last change |
| `3j` | down three lines |
| `u` | undo — itself a change to the buffer, but never what `.` repeats |

@hints
Delete the first `WIP ` with a single operator. Then move to the next one
and ask for the same thing again rather than retyping it.
--
`dw` takes a word and its trailing space in two keys. After that, every
remaining prefix costs you a move and a `.`.
--
Count the gaps: the prefixed lines are two apart, then adjacent, then
three apart. `2j.`, `j.`, `3j.` after the first `dw`.

@solution
    2j  dw  2j.  j.  3j.

Four identical deletions, one of them typed.

What makes this work is vim's definition of a change: anything that
modifies the buffer. Moving, scrolling, searching, `:w`, entering and
leaving a mode without typing — none of those touch the dot register, so
none of them cost you the repeat. That is why `.` is usable in real work
and not just on tidy adjacent lines. You can go and look at something
else, come back, and `.` is still holding your edit.

Two things do overwrite it, and both are fair: making any other change,
and undoing. `u` undoes the change, and if you then press `.` you repeat
the original edit, not the undo. Try it — undo one of the deletions and
press `.` to put it back through the front door.

@start CHANGELOG.md
## Unreleased

WIP refund window now capped at 30 days
Cancelling an order records a reason
WIP receipt email uses the new template
WIP payment retries backed off to three attempts
Customer lookup no longer calls the API twice
Batch reconciliation runs at 02:00
WIP refund webhook verifies its signature

@expected CHANGELOG.md
